# Creative design

The product must feel **exceptionally creative and visually distinctive** — never interchangeable template SaaS, never the AI-default "gradient blob + 3-column features + gray cards" aesthetic.

## What distinction comes from

Intentional **art direction**, not random decoration:

- **Typography** — purposeful type system, real hierarchy, considered pairings.
- **Color** — a system with rhythm and accents, not "primary + gray ramp".
- **Spacing** — a deliberate spatial scale that gives the UI a recognizable cadence.
- **Motion** — subtle, **purposeful** transitions that reinforce state changes; never noisy or decorative.
- **Layout motifs** — recurring compositional moves (asymmetry, framing, anchored elements) that identify the product.

## What to refuse

- Generic gray cards, default Material/Bootstrap shadows, default-button look.
- Stock travel pastiche (postcards, airplane icons everywhere) used **decoratively**. (The postcard motif used **intentionally** to anchor the post composer, pending-review state, and DM treatment — see "Social surfaces" below — is on-brand. Decorative postcard imagery elsewhere is still a refusal.)
- "AI slop" landing pages: hero + 3 columns + testimonials + CTA, in that order, with default rounded buttons.
- Visual novelty that **breaks** legibility, focus, semantics, or forms.

### Social-surface anti-patterns (added 2026-04-18, ADR 0002)

The social MVP introduces feed, composer, profile, follow, comment, reaction, DM, notification, and explore surfaces. Refuse outright on every one of them:

- Generic blue-corporate-rectangle "What's on your mind?" composer.
- Instagram-style flat 3-column image grid for explore.
- Thumbs-up / heart reactions; emoji-pile reaction bars.
- Red-circle notification badges in the chrome.
- iMessage-style chat bubbles with directional tails.
- Material-Design floating action buttons.
- Gradient-blob "social" landing page.
- Default Mapbox / Google blue map style on the explore tab.

If a screen could be screenshotted into a generic-social-app-clone collection without anyone noticing, **it has failed.**

## Hard rule: reference patterns, never copy visuals

> **Reference social UX patterns from Facebook, Instagram, BeReal, Geneva, Partiful, Letterboxd, and Strava freely; never copy their visual treatments. Every social surface must look unmistakably like Jettison or it has failed.**

What this means in practice:

- **Patterns are fair game** — feed structure, composer affordances, follow/unfollow flows, comment threading, notification design, explore filtering, DM thread ergonomics. Borrow what works.
- **Visuals are not** — color palette, typography, iconography, motif language, shape language, copy register, motion grammar all come from Jettison's locked direction (warm/tactile/considered/quietly-confident; Ink/Ember/Paper; Fraunces+Inter+JetBrains Mono; oversized money numerals; layered warm surfaces).

The seven concrete starting sketches that operationalize this rule (postcard composer, trip-grouped feed, custom reaction glyphs, "in the mail" pending state, trip-notes DMs, map-first explore, pennant notifications) live in [`.cursor/skills/project-ui-ux/SKILL.md`](../skills/project-ui-ux/SKILL.md) under "Social surfaces — distinctive by default". This rule and that skill section must stay in sync — touch one, touch the other.

## Brand: Jettison

The public product name is **Jettison**. Direction-level visual identity is locked in [`app-context/product-engineering.md`](../../app-context/product-engineering.md) → "Design direction" — read it before designing any new screen. Summary:

- **Pillars:** trust / warmth / momentum.
- **Mood:** generous, tactile, considered, quietly confident.
- **Signature motif:** oversized money numerals (tabular figures). Other motifs: anchored bottom action bar, layered warm surfaces.
- **Palette character:** warm neutrals + single confident accent (Ember) + earthed semantics. Sample hex values are in `product-engineering.md`; treat them as **direction-level**, not as final brand swatches.
- **Type:** Fraunces (display) + Inter (text) + JetBrains Mono (money tables only) — all Google Fonts (SIL OFL).
- **JTSN role:** secondary mark (favicon / app shortcut / monogram). The full word **Jettison** is the primary wordmark.
- **Voice:** use the name **Jettison** freely in marketing and brand voice. **Do not use "jettison" as a verb in transactional UI** — "Jettison your share" reads as *throw your money away*. Use neutral verbs ("Pay", "Send", "Settle") in product flows.

New screens **extend the established direction** — pillars, mood, motifs — rather than each pulling toward a different mood. If a screen needs a new motif, write the reason in `product-engineering.md` first.

## Tokens

Tokens live in the **`@theme` block in [`app/globals.css`](../../app/globals.css)** (Tailwind v4 idiom). Names map to utility classes — `--color-ink` becomes `text-ink`, `bg-ink`, `border-ink`. There is exactly one source of truth; don't introduce a parallel token file or a Tailwind config object. Raw hex codes never live in components — extend `@theme` first, and if it implies a brand decision, explain it in `product-engineering.md` → "Color" before shipping.

## Reduced motion

Honor `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` (snippet + essential-vs-decorative motion list in `product-engineering.md` → "Motion"). Every animated state change must have a non-motion equivalent (color, copy, icon).

## Non-negotiable

- **WCAG-minded contrast** for text and interactive elements.
- **Visible focus states** on every focusable element — do not strip focus rings to "look clean".
- **Readable type** at mobile sizes (no 11 px body text).
- Coordinate with `mobile-web-ui.mdc`: creativity must not break tap targets, hover/touch parity, or safe areas.

If a visual idea fights usability, **change the execution** (layout, weight, hierarchy), not the requirement for clarity.
